{
  "id": "2026-10-05-google-redaction-slip",
  "title": "Google tried to hide how much water its data centers use. A copy and paste gave it away.",
  "topic": "테크",
  "level": 2,
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
  "sentences": [
    "Good morning!",
    "Here's a story about a very old mistake with very new numbers.",
    "In Nebraska, data centers now have to report how much water and electricity they use.",
    "Google filed its reports, but it blacked out the numbers, calling them trade secrets.",
    "There was just one problem.",
    "The black boxes only covered the text on the screen.",
    "A local TV station highlighted the boxes, copied them, and pasted the hidden numbers into another document.",
    "So what did they find?",
    "Google's site in Lincoln uses about thirteen million gallons of water a year, roughly twenty Olympic swimming pools.",
    "Across six data centers in the state, the total comes to around seven hundred and sixty-five million gallons.",
    "The station also found that Google expects tens of millions of dollars in tax refunds.",
    "That's why local residents want answers.",
    "They're asking whether these big facilities give back as much as they take.",
    "For engineers, the lesson is simple.",
    "Covering something up on the surface is not the same as removing it.",
    "So the next time you share a document, double-check what's really underneath."
  ],
  "summary_ko": [
    "네브래스카주가 데이터센터에 물·전기 사용량 보고를 의무화했고, 구글은 숫자를 영업비밀이라며 가려서 냈다.",
    "그런데 검은 상자가 글자 위만 덮고 있어서, 지역 방송국이 복사·붙여넣기만으로 숫자를 다 읽어냈다 (링컨 시설만 연 1,300만 갤런).",
    "주민들은 수천만 달러 세금 환급을 받는 시설이 그만큼 돌려주는지 묻고 있고, 개발자에겐 '겉을 가리는 것 ≠ 지우는 것'이라는 교훈이다."
  ],
